course: politics
lesson: 26
title: Israel and Gaza: what happened, as widely reported
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Two peoples, Israeli Jews and Palestinians, see the same small land between the Jordan River and the Mediterranean Sea as home, and they have fought over it for more than seventy years. Each people tells the story of how it began in its own way.
explain: On 7 October 2023 fighters from Hamas, the armed group that ran Gaza, attacked the south of Israel; news reports said about 1,200 people were killed, most of them ordinary people, and about 250 were taken to Gaza as hostages.
explain: Israel then bombed Gaza and sent in soldiers to fight Hamas and bring the hostages home. Gaza's Hamas-run health ministry reported tens of thousands of deaths, many of them women and children, and news reports said most homes were damaged or destroyed.
explain: Picture two children: one in a shelter room in Israel as a siren wails, one in a tent in Gaza holding his only toy. Both are frightened and both families are grieving. You can feel for both, and you can say which points are agreed and which are contested.
explain: Some words are fought over: South Africa accused Israel of genocide at the UN's top court, and some UN experts and rights groups used the word; Israel and its supporters strongly reject it and call the war self-defence against Hamas. The court had not given its final ruling at the time of writing.
explain: Words from the scenes: «kicked off» = started; «me» (Ireland) = my, «yer man» = that man, «eejit» = idiot; «cap» = a lie; «bairn» (Scotland) = child, «wi'» = with, «pure» = really, «dreich» = grey and miserable.
fact: On 7 October 2023 Hamas fighters broke into southern Israel; news reports said about 1,200 people were killed, most of them civilians, and about 250 were taken to Gaza as hostages.
fact: Gaza is a small, crowded strip of land by the sea between Israel and Egypt, home to about two million Palestinians.
fact: After 7 October Israel's army bombed Gaza and sent in soldiers to fight Hamas and free the hostages; news reports said most homes were damaged or destroyed and almost everyone had to flee at least once.
fact: Gaza's health ministry, part of the Hamas-run government, reported tens of thousands of deaths, many of them women and children; Israel disputed the counts and said thousands of the dead were fighters, while the UN and researchers said the ministry's counts in past wars had proved fairly close.
fact: UN agencies reported severe hunger in Gaza, and in August 2025 food experts used by the UN said there was famine in Gaza City; Israel disputed this and said it let in a lot of food and Hamas stole aid.
fact: Hostages' families campaigned to bring them home; some hostages were freed in deals, a few were rescued by soldiers, and some died while held, news reports said.
fact: A one-week pause came in November 2023; a ceasefire began in January 2025 and broke down in March; another began in October 2025 under a US-backed plan, when the living hostages came home and many Palestinian prisoners were freed, though deadly incidents were still reported afterwards.
fact: During the war rockets and missiles were fired at Israel from Gaza, by Hezbollah in Lebanon, by the Houthis in Yemen and by Iran, and many Israeli families had to leave their homes for months, news reports said.
fact: Most schools in Gaza were damaged or used as shelters, and many families lived in tents with little clean water, news reports said.
fact: South Africa accused Israel of genocide, trying to destroy a whole people, at the International Court of Justice, the UN's top court; Israel strongly rejected the claim, and the case was expected to take years.
fact: Israel was founded in 1948; Jewish people celebrate its independence, while Palestinians mourn the Nakba, «the catastrophe», when hundreds of thousands fled or were forced from their homes.
fact: In 1967, after a war, Israel took control of the West Bank and Gaza.
fact: The best-known peace idea is two states, Israel and Palestine, side by side; talks have failed many times, and some Israeli and Palestinian families who lost children meet to talk together.
exercise: What happened on 7 October 2023, as widely reported? | Hamas fighters attacked southern Israel, killing about 1,200 people and taking about 250 hostages | Israel and the Palestinians signed a peace deal in Cairo, after many years of talks led by the United Nations | A storm flooded the whole of Gaza | Egypt's army invaded southern Israel
exercise: What is Hamas? | The armed Palestinian group that ran Gaza | Israel's army | A UN aid agency | A city in Egypt
exercise: What is Gaza? | A small, crowded strip of land by the sea, home to about two million Palestinians | A large desert country in the middle of Africa, home to about two hundred million people | An island in the Pacific Ocean | A city in the far north of Israel
exercise: What did Israel do after 7 October? | Bombed Gaza and sent in soldiers to fight Hamas and bring the hostages home | Did nothing at all | Moved all of its own people into Gaza and gave the whole of Israel to Egypt to look after | Handed Gaza over to Egypt
exercise: Why does Aisha see two different claims about the death toll in Gaza? | Gaza's Hamas-run ministry counted, and Israel disputes the counts and says many were fighters | Nobody has counted anything | Both claims are about a football score from a match played in Gaza, and the reporters mixed them up | The UN made up both numbers
exercise: What do most people agree about the deaths in Gaza? | A very large number of people died, many of them children | Nobody died at all | Only fighters died | Every number is exactly agreed
exercise: What did food experts used by the UN say in August 2025? | There was famine in Gaza City | Gaza had far too much food to eat | There had never been any hunger in Gaza | Food prices had fallen to zero there
exercise: What did Israel say about the hunger reports? | It disputed them and said it let food in and Hamas stole aid | It said there was never any war at all, and that the pictures were made up | It said it had sent no food on purpose, to win the war faster | It said the UN controls Israel's border and decides what goes in
exercise: Whose faces are on the «Kidnapped» posters Mateo sees? | People taken hostage by Hamas on 7 October | Missing pets | Footballers on strike | Actors in a new film
exercise: What does a yellow ribbon tied to a fence mean here? | Hoping someone comes home safely | A road is closed | A shop is having a sale | A birthday party is nearby
exercise: What happened to the ceasefire that began in January 2025? | It broke down in March and the fighting started again | It lasted forever and is still holding today, with no fighting | It ended the conflict for good and every soldier went home | Nobody noticed it at all, because the news never reported it
exercise: Why did Hannah's cousin in Israel run to a shelter room? | A siren warned of rockets | It was a fire drill for a party | She was playing hide and seek | The internet had stopped working
exercise: What happened to many schools in Gaza? | They were damaged or used as shelters for families | They became football stadiums | They closed only for the summer | They were moved to Egypt
exercise: What does «genocide» mean? | Trying to destroy a whole people | Any war between two countries | A kind of medicine | A peace agreement
exercise: Finn's grandad says 1948, his aunt 1967 and his cousin 7 October. Why the different answers? | Each starts the story at a different big event | Two of them cannot count | Only 1948 really happened | The dates are made up
exercise: What is the «two-state solution»? | The idea of Israel and Palestine as two countries side by side | Moving everyone from both peoples to two faraway countries instead | Splitting Gaza into two football teams that play in two leagues | Giving Egypt two capital cities, one in Cairo and one in Gaza City
test: Finn in Dublin says: «Grandad says it all kicked off in 1948, me cousin says 7 October, yer man on the telly says 1967 — so who's the eejit?» What is the best answer? | None of them; each picks a real turning point, and where you start changes the story | Grandad, because 1948 never happened | The cousin, because nothing at all happened between those peoples before 2023, so the older dates don't count | The man on TV, because TV is always wrong
test: One kid says: «the Gaza death number is cap, it's from Hamas.» Another says: «no, it's real.» What is the careful reply? | It comes from the Hamas-run ministry and Israel disputes it, but the UN says past counts were fairly close, and most agree very many died | It is a lie and nobody died | Everyone agrees on the exact number, because the UN and Israel and Hamas all counted the dead together and got exactly the same figure | It comes from a video game
test: A girl in Edinburgh says: «that bairn in Gaza lives in a tent wi' one toy, it's pure dreich.» What would help her understand his story? | His school became a shelter, and many families had to move again and again | He chose a tent because camping is fun and his family wanted a holiday by the sea | Gaza has never had any houses | Every child in the world lives in a tent
test: Someone online sneers: «the hostages? shut up, they're made up for the news.» What is true? | About 250 people were taken; some were freed in deals, some rescued, and some died while held | Nobody was ever taken | They were all freed the very next day, after one phone call between the leaders, and were home by teatime | They were soldiers on a holiday trip
test: What would most likely happen to a ceasefire if each side kept saying the other broke it? | It could break down, as the January 2025 one did in March | It would last forever automatically, whatever either side said | It would turn into a full, signed peace treaty within a week | It would become a law in Ireland and in every other country
test: Ethan's uncle says «genocide» and his aunt says «self-defence». Which sentence reports this fairly? | South Africa accused Israel of genocide at the UN's top court; Israel rejects it and calls its war self-defence | Everyone agrees it is genocide | Everyone agrees it is only self-defence | The court gave its final ruling last week, and both the uncle and the aunt were told they are wrong and must stop
test: In a cartoon a wizard ends the conflict by drawing one line across the map. What is the real situation? | Many peace talks have failed, because land, safety and fairness are hard to agree | A line on a map always ends a war, as long as both leaders sign it with the same pen | The conflict ended because of a wizard | Nobody has ever tried peace talks
test: Hannah's cousin runs to a shelter; Zara sees a boy in a tent in Gaza. What do both stories show? | Children on both sides are frightened and suffering | Only Israeli children are afraid | Only Palestinian children are afraid | Neither child is really affected
test: Noah in Ohio asks: «Gaza's a country, right? Like Canada?» What is the best answer? | It is a small strip of land where about two million Palestinians live, next to Israel and Egypt | Yes, and it is bigger than Canada, with more than a hundred million people living in its cities and forests | No, it is a city in Ohio | It is an island in the Pacific
test: Ruby stops eating after the soup clip. Which statement fits the reports? | Most agree children went hungry; people dispute why — limits on aid, or Hamas and the fighting | Nobody in Gaza was hungry | Gaza had more food than Australia | The hunger was caused by the soup itself, because the soup in the clip had gone bad and made people ill
test: Priya asks: «Why can't they just share?» Which answer is true? | Two-state peace talks failed many times, but some families who lost children on both sides still meet to talk | Sharing has already worked perfectly | Nobody has ever suggested sharing the land, not once, in all the many years since the conflict between them began | It is illegal to talk about peace
exam: Select the response consistent with the established reports concerning the events of 7 October 2023. | Fighters of Hamas entered southern Israel, whereupon approximately twelve hundred persons were killed and approximately two hundred and fifty taken hostage | Israeli and Palestinian leaders concluded a treaty of peace in Cairo, whereupon all hostilities ceased and every person detained by either party was released forthwith | A natural disaster inundated the Gaza Strip | No persons were killed or detained
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the casualty figures published by the Gaza health ministry? | They are issued by a ministry of the Hamas-run government, are disputed by Israel, and in past conflicts were judged by the UN to have been fairly close | They are accepted by all parties without any qualification | They were compiled by the Israeli army, which publishes them daily together with its own estimates of the number of fighters killed in the course of the conflict | They record that no persons died
exam: Identify the significance attributed to the year 1948 by each of the two peoples. | Israelis commemorate independence, whereas Palestinians mourn the Nakba | Both peoples commemorate it as the founding of Egypt | Both peoples regard it as the end of all conflict between them and their neighbours | Neither people attaches any significance to it
exam: State the allegation brought by South Africa before the International Court of Justice. | That Israel's conduct in Gaza constitutes genocide, an allegation which Israel rejects | That Hamas constitutes a member state of the United Nations, entitled to vote in the General Assembly | That Egypt had annexed the Gaza Strip | That the court ought to relocate to Gaza
